Internal mic: use the mixer's level instead of OS X's fixed -12 dB

cs_8409_intmike_volume_setup() is called at boot and at every capture start with the gain OS X uses, 0x27
(-12 dB; the amp's 0 dB is 0x33, its maximum 0x3f = +12 dB, 1 dB steps). It writes the codec directly, so the
"Internal Mic Capture Volume" control, which PipeWire sets to its maximum, kept showing +12 dB while the codec
recorded at -12 dB: speech came in about 24 dB too quiet and the mixer had no effect.

Take the gain from the control's cached value (snd_hda_codec_amp_read) instead. The mixer then works, and the
default level is the control's. Tested on a MacBookPro13,2 (CS8409/CS42L83), kernel 7.2.5.

For github.com/davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro (and the egorenar fork), patch_cirrus/patch_cirrus_real84.h.
Applied by scripts/install-sound.sh (patch -p1 in the driver's source folder).
---
--- a/patch_cirrus/patch_cirrus_real84.h
+++ b/patch_cirrus/patch_cirrus_real84.h
@@ -286,9 +286,17 @@
 {
         int retgain;
         int newgain;
+        int mixer;
 
         struct cs8409_apple_spec *spec = codec->spec;
 
+        // Use the level of the "Internal Mic Capture Volume" mixer control (its cached value) instead of the
+        // fixed gain OS X uses (0x27, -12 dB). With the fixed gain the mic was far too quiet and the mixer had
+        // no effect: it showed up to +12 dB while the codec stayed at -12 dB from every capture start on.
+        mixer = snd_hda_codec_amp_read(codec, spec->intmike_adc_nid, 0, HDA_INPUT, 0);
+        if (mixer >= 0)
+                volume = mixer & HDA_AMP_VOLMASK;
+
         // plausibly AppleHDAWidget::setWidgetAmplifierGain
 
         //retgain = snd_hda_codec_read_check(codec, 0x22, 0, AC_VERB_GET_AMP_GAIN_MUTE, 0x00002000, 0x00000033, 0); // 0x022b2000
